# regexp-replace var regexp replace
#
# A global search and replace over the string a *named* variable holds, with
# POSIX extended regular expressions.  The variable is changed in place; the
# status is 0 when something was replaced and 1 when nothing matched.
#
# Written from zshcontrib(1) and from what the installed shell answers, never
# from its function files.  docs/spec/functions.md records the run.
#
# The replacement is expanded, once, per match: parameter, command and
# arithmetic expansions in it are done then, which is what makes `$MATCH` —
# the text this match consumed — the useful thing to write in one.  `$match`,
# `$mbegin` and `$mend` are the groups, and they are the regular expression
# code's parameters rather than this function's, which is why the manual says
# not to keep anything of your own in them.  They are declared local here, so
# a caller's own `$MATCH` survives the call.
#
# Measured on zsh 5.9.2, and each of these is a case the loop below is shaped
# by:
#
#   aaa   `a*` -> X        X            a match that eats the string
#   abc   `x*` -> Y        YaYbYc       an empty match advances by one
#   abcabc `b*` -> -       -a--c-a--c   both at once, and no match past the end
#   hello `z`  -> X        unchanged, status 1
#
# The empty match is the whole of why this is a loop over a shrinking subject
# rather than one pass: a pattern that can match nothing matches *between*
# every pair of characters, and a loop that did not step over one would not
# end.
#
# `^` anchors what is left of the subject rather than the original string, so
# `regexp-replace v '^a' Z` over `aXaXa` replaces the first `a` and stops.
# That is measured, and zshcontrib(1) warns of it in the same words: with
# POSIX EREs the `^` and word-boundary operators may not work as expected.

emulate -L zsh

local variable=$1 expression=$2 replacement=$3
local MATCH MBEGIN MEND
local -a match mbegin mend
local subject result piece
integer replaced=0

subject=${(P)variable}

while [[ -n $subject ]]; do
	[[ $subject =~ $expression ]] || break
	replaced=1
	result+=${subject[1,MBEGIN-1]}
	eval "piece=\"$replacement\""
	result+=$piece
	if (( MEND < MBEGIN )); then
		# An empty match. Carry the character it sat in front of over
		# untouched and go on from the next one, or the same position would
		# match again forever.
		result+=${subject[MBEGIN]}
		subject=${subject[MBEGIN+1,-1]}
	else
		subject=${subject[MEND+1,-1]}
	fi
done

(( replaced )) || return 1
typeset -g "$variable"="${result}${subject}"
return 0
